   "cite": "efficiency",
   "prompt": "<p>A Carnot engine works between reservoirs at 500 K and 300 K and takes in $\\kqh = 1000$ J per cycle at the hot side. Find (a) its efficiency $\\eta$, (b) the work of the cycle $\\kwcycle$ (work done on the gas), and (c) the heat $\\kqc$ the gas takes in at the cold side. (d) The same cycle is run backwards as a heat pump that delivers 1000 J to the warm side. How much work must be put in?</p>",
   "answer": {
    "type": "multi",
    "generated_by": "ai",
    "parts": [
     {
      "part": "a",
      "value": 0.4,
      "hint": "Use Equation 20.7.1."
     },
     {
      "part": "b",
      "value": -400,
      "unit": "J"
     },
     {
      "part": "c",
      "value": -600,
      "unit": "J"
     },
     {
      "part": "d",
      "value": 400,
      "unit": "J"
     }
    ],
    "solution": "<p>(a) $\\eta = 1 - \\kTc/\\kTh = 1 - 300/500 = 0.40$. (b) $|\\kwcycle| = \\eta\\,\\kqh = 400$ J, and the gas does this work on the surroundings, so $\\kwcycle = -400$ J. (c) From $\\kqh + \\kqc = -\\kwcycle$, $\\kqc = 400 - 1000 = -600$ J: 600 J leaves the gas at 300 K. The ratio checks: $\\kqc/\\kqh = -0.60 = -\\kTc/\\kTh$. (d) Reversed, every flow changes sign: 400 J of work and 600 J pumped from the cold side together deliver 1000 J to the warm side, so the work put in is 400 J.</p>"
   }
  }
 ],
