 "footnotes": [
  {
   "id": "c-whot",
   "kind": "correction",
   "html": "In the book's convention $w$ is the work done on the gas, so $w_{hot}$ is negative and $w_{cold}$ positive. What is meant is $|w_{hot}| > |w_{cold}|$: the gas does more work expanding at $T_h$ than is done on it while it is compressed at $T_c$."
  },
  {
   "id": "c-24",
   "kind": "correction",
   "html": "$1 - 300/400 = 0.25$, so the best efficiency is 25%, which agrees with the three quarters of the energy dumped in the river."
  },
  {
   "id": "c-stirling-order",
   "kind": "correction",
   "html": "Figure 20.7.4 takes the steps in the other order: after the expansion at $T_h$ (A to B), step 2 is isochoric cooling from $T_h$ to $T_c$ (B to C), and step 4 is isochoric heating from $T_c$ to $T_h$ (D to A)."
  },
  {
   "id": "c-stirling-q",
   "kind": "correction",
   "html": "The heat of an isochoric step is $C_V\\Delta T$, not $C_V/\\Delta T$: with $\\Delta T = T_h - T_c$, Equations 20.7.2 and 20.7.3 read $q_2 = C_V\\Delta T$ and $q_4 = -C_V\\Delta T$."
  }
 ],
